    "title": "Concept: The Carbon Cycle",
    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">The carbon cycle moves carbon through living organisms, the atmosphere, oceans, and soil. Plants remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere by <strong>photosynthesis</strong>. Organisms release carbon dioxide back via <strong>respiration</strong> and when fossil fuels or biomass burn in <strong>combustion</strong>. Decomposers break down dead material, returning carbon to the soil and atmosphere.</p>",
    "analogy": {
      "title": "Analogy",
      "content": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">Imagine the carbon cycle like money in an economy: carbon moves between banks (atmosphere), businesses (plants), consumers (animals), and recyclers (decomposers), ensuring carbon is reused continuously.</p>"
    },
